dzn: Poll a fence for a while before blocking on it

Under WSL2 a wait that blocks in dzn_sync_wait() is an RPC to the Windows
host: SetEventOnMultipleFenceCompletion becomes the graphics kernel's
wait-for-synchronization-object ioctl (dxgkio 0x3a), and with GPU work in
flight it took 6 to 15 ms to return (8 ms on average), long after the
fence's completed value, which is readable without a kernel call, showed
the work done: 0.15 ms after an empty submission on an RTX 4080 Laptop GPU
and on an Intel UHD Graphics, where vkQueueWaitIdle took 6.6 ms. While
Chrome drew at 10 frames a second in Android 16, crosvm made 70 such calls
a second. The calls also hold up the other graphics-kernel calls of the
process, which took 1-2 ms each instead of 0.1 ms.

Poll the completed values before creating the event and blocking: yield for
the first 300 us, then sleep 50 us between polls, for up to 50 ms. A wait on
longer work still blocks, after that much polling. DZN_SPIN_US sets the 50
ms in microseconds (0 turns the poll off), DZN_SPIN_YIELD_US the 300 us and
DZN_SPIN_SLEEP_US the 50 us. Native Windows does not poll.

An empty submission followed by a wait took a median 0.15 ms instead of 6.2
ms (90th percentile 0.24 instead of 9.6 ms). In Android 16 on the RTX 4080
under WSL2, with two runs each: scrolling the Settings app's list had a
median frame of 35 ms instead of 115 ms and 33% of frames janky instead of
99%, 538 frames rendered in the run instead of 223; a WebGL page drew 17.5
to 19 frames a second instead of 9.5; the Settings app started in 2.78 s
instead of 3.38 s. The host used 0.17 core more while scrolling. A poll of
1 ms gave a median frame of 99 ms, one of 20 ms 36 ms, and 200 ms nothing
more than 50 ms.

